{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"For tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the non-expert panel-plus-checklist will on average produce more reliable judgments because aggregating independent judgments cancels uncorrelated noise—a structural advantage the single expert cannot replicate internally.","counterconditions":["Panelist errors are positively correlated (shared checklist misreading, common training bias) so aggregation cancels far less noise than assumed.","The task's true outcome depends on non-linear cue interactions (SRC_008) that an additive checklist cannot encode, capping panel ceiling below expert ceiling.","The outcome distribution is highly skewed (SRC_006); checklist has high implicit base-rate prior and panel merely tracks base rate, not signal."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A checklist decomposes the task into atomic, verifiable items. Each non-expert's judgment becomes an independent draw with item-specific noise. Averaging across panelists cancels idiosyncratic errors (Condorcet / measurement theory), leaving the signal. The expert's intuition is a single draw; even if its mean is well-calibrated, its variance on any one case is irreducible. The panel's standard error shrinks as 1/√n.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","63f71ce7d4da20778df37cc61c493f02e48ba07f2b11c42f416ad196cd74288e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The claim is false in general: the expert's intuition is itself an implicit, internalized checklist (latent variable model). If the expert's internal items are well-calibrated, the panel's explicit aggregation adds no marginal reliability; the debate collapses to explicit aggregation vs. implicit integration, and the latter can win.","counterconditions":["Expert intuition is shown to have systematically higher variance than panel aggregate on the same tasks (expert is overconfident or noisy).","The expert's implicit checklist is poorly calibrated—miscalibrated cue weights—so the implicit integration is worse than explicit aggregation.","Panel checklist is shown to encode non-linear cue interactions (e.g., via conditional items), removing the additive-ceiling objection."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is an implicit psychometric decomposition of the task—internal cues with learned weights. If the expert's implicit checklist has high internal reliability (low noise on each latent cue, well-calibrated integration), its single integrated output can have lower variance than an aggregate of noisy non-experts whose only advantage is cancellation of uncorrelated error. When cue interactions are non-linear (SRC_008), the expert's implicit integration handles them; the additive explicit checklist cannot.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","63f71ce7d4da20778df37cc61c493f02e48ba07f2b11c42f416ad196cd74288e","a9f73d70338bb7425aba3bf94501ecfb85cc81c22436eaf446c018ea3e772eeb"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The apparent reliability gain of the non-expert panel is often a baseline-rate artifact: on highly skewed outcome distributions, a checklist with high implicit base-rate prior produces high accuracy by tracking the majority class, not by discriminating signal.","counterconditions":["Evaluation uses balanced accuracy or area under ROC curve, not raw accuracy; panel's apparent gain disappears under discrimination metrics.","Outcome distribution is not skewed; panel's gain persists on balanced datasets.","Checklist items are shown to have criterion validity independent of base rate (panel discriminates minority cases)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklists encode surface cues correlated with the majority outcome. When outcomes are skewed (e.g., 90% negative), a panel that follows checklist cues consistent with the base rate will score ~90% accuracy while being completely non-discriminative on the minority cases. The single expert may score lower raw accuracy but higher discrimination (sensitivity/specificity balanced). The 'reliability gain' is an artifact of scoring accuracy on a skewed distribution rather than discrimination.","optional_refs":["e95cb4f1c4ca2abacfd3e1befbd2fc5ee9cd4867bfee105781b8883620e7a02a","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Within a single evaluation, genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere checklist compliance by introducing a placebo (decoy) checklist high in compliance-cues but low in task-validity. If the panel's accuracy remains high under the placebo, the original gain was compliance, not discrimination.","counterconditions":["No placebo arm was included in the evaluation; there is no within-evaluation way to distinguish compliance from discrimination.","The placebo checklist inadvertently encodes some valid cues, contaminating the control.","Panelists recognize the placebo as invalid and abandon it, making the placebo condition non-informative."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The evaluation assigns one sub-panel the valid checklist and another a matched decoy checklist with superficially plausible but criterion-invalid items. Both sub-panels produce judgments. If accuracy is similar across both, the panel is tracking checklist surface features (compliance), not task signal. If accuracy drops sharply under the placebo, the valid checklist provided genuine discriminative signal. This is the placebo-control logic imported from clinical trials, applied to checklists.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.25},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Within a single evaluation, genuine reliability gain is distinguished from compliance by checking item-level conditionality: if the panel's correctness on the final outcome is independent of correctness on intermediate checklist items, the panel is merely pattern-matching the final answer, not executing a discriminative reasoning chain.","counterconditions":["Intermediate items are not actually intermediate (they are redundant with the final item), making conditional dependence trivially high without implying discrimination.","Panelists get intermediate items right and final item right but for the wrong causal reason (common-cause confound inflates conditional dependence without genuine discrimination).","The checklist items are not causally ordered, so the conditional independence test is meaningless."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Log a per-item correctness vector for each panelist. Compute the conditional dependence: P(outcome_correct | intermediate_items_correct) vs P(outcome_correct | intermediate_items_wrong). Genuine discrimination implies strong conditional dependence—getting intermediate items right should predict getting the outcome right. Mere compliance implies independence—panelists may get the final answer right (tracking base rate) while getting intermediate items wrong, or vice versa. The conditional structure distinguishes a reasoning chain from surface-pattern matching.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Within a single evaluation, the decisive evidence is whether panel-level error reduction is concentrated on cases where the expert is wrong, versus merely on easy cases. Genuine gain means the panel catches expert errors; mere compliance means the panel merely agrees with the expert on easy cases and adds nothing on hard ones.","counterconditions":["Expert and panel errors are perfectly correlated (shared systematic blind spot), so panel never catches expert errors—gain is zero on hard cases.","Panel outperforms expert on hard cases but via a different systematic bias that happens to anti-correlate with expert bias; the 'correction' is not genuine discrimination but bias-cancellation.","Difficulty stratification is post-hoc and overfits the evaluation set; the interaction does not generalize."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Stratify cases by expert confidence or difficulty. Compute panel accuracy conditional on expert-wrong. If the panel's aggregate reliability gain is driven by cases where the expert erred (error-correcting), the gain is genuine discrimination. If the gain is driven by cases where both panel and expert are right (easy cases, base rate), the gain is compliance with surface cues correlated with easy outcomes. The within-evidence signature is the interaction term: panel_vs_expert × difficulty.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","140af47df69a2e4c13b2217c7a8b90c9cbdbe8526f58355ebfda06d54a05f27d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45}],"simulation_proposals":[]}